    "ls|LS16_S17_U_161": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 161 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2019 \n \nNational Institute of Science \n \n161. SHRI ARVIND SAWANT:  \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the National Institute of Science, Bangalore has been recognized in the top 10 \nscience research institutes in the world and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) whether the institute has demanded a financial aid to the tune of crores of rupees for \ndevelopment of indigenous nano technology and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c) the action taken by the Government to provide financial assistance to this institute? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(DR. SATYA PAL SINGH) \n \n(a) to (c): There is no Institute named as National Institute of Science in Bangalore under \nGovernment of  India.  There is an Indi an Institute of Science (IISc) at Bangalore \nnotified as an Institute of Eminence (IoE) ranked 170 in the QS World University \nRanking 2019.  A total amount of Rs. 17.00 crore has been released to IISc Bangalore \nfor Centre for Nano Science & Engineering (CeNSE) consisting of Rs. 5.00 crore in \n2017-18 and Rs. 12.00 crore so far in 2018-19.   \n \n \n* * * * * \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S17_U_162": " \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE\n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.162\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 4.2.2019\n \n \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF MYTHOLOGICAL CULTURAL \n \nHERITAGES\n \nOF MITHILA REGION\n \n \n+162.\n \nSHRI KIRTI AZAD:\n \n \n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state: \n \n \n(a)\n \nthe details of the mythological\n-\nhistorical\n-\ncultural heritages of Mithila \nregion of Bihar which are being developed at present; and\n \n \n(b)\n \nthe steps taken by the Government for development of these heritages?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CH\nARGE) FOR CULTURE AND MINISTER \nOF STATE FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE\n \n \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA)\n \n \n \n(a)&(b)\n \nIn Mithila region “Remains of Ancient Fort or Garh” locally known as \nRaja Bali ka Garh, Balirajgarh, district Madhubani in Bihar is a \ncentrall\ny protected monument under Archaeological Survey of India.   \nAnnual maintenance work at the monument is carried out and at \npresent construction of boundary wall is in progress.\n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S17_U_163": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 163 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2019 \n \nOPENING OF EMPLOYEES’ INSURANCE CORPORATION HOSPITALS \nFOR GENERAL PUBLIC \n \n163. SHRIMATI MEENAKASHI LEKHI:  \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n  \n(a)whether the Government proposes to open the hospitals under the \nEmployees’ State Insurance Corporation for general public and if so, \nthe details thereof;  \n(b)whether all the hospitals are  sufficiently staffed to take on the \nadditional patient load and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c)whether any generic medicine shops under Pradhan Mantri Jan -\nAushadhi Yojana have been set up near such hospitals to help the \ngeneral public to avail afford able medicines and if so, the details \nthereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n \n(a) & (b): The primary mandate of Employees’ State Insurance \nCorporation (ESIC) is to provide social security benefits, including \nmedical benefits to the Insured Persons covered under the ESIC \nSchemes. The ESI Corporation  in its 175 th /176th  meeting held on \n18.09.2018/05.12.2018 has taken a decision for providing access to \nNon-Insured Persons for medical services in its u nder-utilized ESIC \nHospitals at Alwar (Rajasthan), Bihta (Bihar), Gulbarga (Karnataka),  \nBareilly and Varanasi (Uttar Pradesh), on user charge basis. \nContd..2/- \n\n::  2  :: \n     The facility of medical service to General Public (Non \nInsured Persons) are bein g extended only in hospitals where medical \nservices are under-utilized. \n(c):  The ESI beneficiaries are provided cashless services, therefore, \nno generic medicine shops have been set up under Pradhan Mantri \nJan-Aushadhi Yojana in the ESIC Hospitals/Dispensaries. \nHowever, as informed by Ministry of Chemicals and Fertilizers, \nfollowing 3 Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi Pariyojana (PMBJP) \nKendras are functional near ESIC Hospitals:-  \n1. KOTHAYALASA, VIZAG Road, Vizianagaram, Andhra Pradesh \n2.  Mettur Dam, Salem, Tamil Nadu \n3.  Mettur, Salem, Tamil Nadu. \n \n******* ",
    "ls|LS16_S17_U_164": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE) \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 164 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON  04th FEBRUARY, 2019 \n \n \nGEMS AND JEWELLERY DOMESTIC COUNCIL \n \n164.  DR. KAMBHAMPATI HARIBABU: \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (वाͨणÏय एवं उɮयोग मंğी ) be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government had constituted a Committee to draft guidelines for \nforming domestic council for Gems and Jewellery industry; \n(b) if so, the details of the mandate given to the said Committee;  \n(c) the details of the manner in which the Government is planning to boost domestic as \nwell as international markets of India’s precious stones; and \n(d) the details of benefits/ encouragement being considered by the Government to \nextend the same to the domestic and jewellery sector? \n \nANSWER \n \nokf.kT; ,oa m|ksx ea=ky; esa jkT; ea=h ¼Jh lh- vkj- pkS/kjh½  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY  \n (SHRI C. R. CHAUDHARY)  \n \n(a) & (b) An Ad-hoc National Committee of Domestic Council for Gems and \nJewellery consisting 14 Members representing various associations of Gems and \nJewellery Sector has been constituted to draft Memorandum and Article of \nAssociation and other Rules/Bye-laws of the Domestic Council. \n(c) & (d)      The government is supporting the process of setting up of Domestic \nCouncil for Gems and Jewellery, to encourage and boost domestic gems and \njewellery sector.  It is envisaged that the domestic industry would be benefited from \nsuch a representative body which would be the forum for highlighting the issues and \nconcerns of the industry that require redressal through policy measures.  Further, \nwith a view to strengthen the Gems & Jewellery industry in the country, the \nGovernment has taken a number of steps such as establishment of Special Notified \nZone (SNZ); setting up of Common Facility Centres for gem and jewellery sector; \nannouncement of separate ITC HS Code for lab-grown diamonds; and providing \nfinancial assistance for participation in international fairs, organizing buyer-seller \nmeets, creation of export related infrastructure, etc. under various schemes of the \nDepartment of Commerce to boost the industry. \n**** ",
    "ls|LS16_S17_U_165": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 165 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2019 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF SPORTS UNDER SKILL INDIA INITIATIVE \n \n165. SHRI BHEEMRAO B. PATIL: \n       \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP be \npleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has taken any initiativ e for the development of sports \nunder the Skill India Initiative;  \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c)  whether the Government has taken any steps in consonance with the \nrecommendations of the National Skill Development C ouncil of the skill gaps in the \nsectors; and  \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof?  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI ANANTKUMAR HEGDE) \n \n(a) to (d)  The Ministry of Skill Development and E ntrepreneurship (MSDE) is \nimplementing its flagship scheme Pradhan Mantri Kau shal Vikas Yojna (PMKVY), \nlaunched in 2015, to provide employable skills to t he youth of the nation. Owing to the \nsuccess of PMKVY 1.0, the scheme was re-launched in  October, 2016 and called \nPMKVY (2016-2020). Under PMKVY (2016-2020), trainin g is provided in more than 250 \njob roles across various sectors including sports. Qualification Packs that have been \ndeveloped for the sports sector are Fitness Trainer , Life Guard-Pool & Beach, Sports \nMasseur, Physical Activity Trainer. Further, MSDE t hrough National Skill Development \nCorporation (NSDC) has formed the Sports, Physical Education, Fitness & Leisure Skill \nCouncil (SPEFL-SC) on 3rd July, 2015 for the focuse d attention to the development of \nsports under the Skill India Initiative. The key ob jective of the SPEFL-SC is to create a \nrobust and vibrant eco-system for quality education  and skill development in the sports \nsector in the country. The mandate for SPEFL-SC, in ter alia, is to carry out a functional \nand occupational mapping and develop a catalogue of  industry, develop occupations/ \njob roles in the sector, develop & put in place an assessment & certification mechanism \nfor the trainers, assessors and trainees etc. NSDC has not conducted any skill gap \nstudy on Sports as yet. \n \n******* ",
    "ls|LS16_S17_U_166": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \nDEPARTMENT FOR PROMOTION OF INDUSTRY AND INTERNAL TRADE \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 166. \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, THE 4TH FEBRUARY, 2019. \n \nTASK FORCE ON e-COMMERCE \n \n166.  SHRI G. HARI: \nDR. K. GOPAL: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY be pleased to state: \n                                           वाणिज्य एवं उद्योग मंत्री \n(a) whether a panel of senior  bureaucrats had reviewed recommendations  of a \ntask force on e-Commerce and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government is  making efforts to hammer out consensus  on the \ne-Commerce Policy and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether a task force set up for  the e -Commerce had drawn criticism for  its \nsuggestions, including making necessary  policy changes to enable founders \nof domestic e -Commerce companies to have  a control even if their share \nholding is small,  allowing foreign direct investment in  e-Retailers that hold \ninventory of all locally  produced items and asking firms to store  data locally \nwithin two years and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d) whether curbs on deep discounts  and bulk purchases of certain items, etc.  \nhave also been sticking points with some  stakeholders and if so, the details \nthereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nवाणिज्‍ य‍एवं‍उद्योग मंत्रालय‍में‍राज्‍ यमंत्री‍(श्री सी.आर.‍चौधरी)‍ \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(SHRI C.R. CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a) to (d):  A Think Tank on ‘Framework for National Policy on e -Commerce’ was \nconstituted by Department of Commerce and a Task Force under the \nThink Tank was set up for preparing recommendations for India’s \nNational Policy on e -Commerce. Department of Commerce formulated \na draft policy document titled “Electronic Commerce in India: \nStakeholder Recommendations Received for a N ational Policy \nFramework” after consulting various stakeholders including concerned \nMinistries/ Departments, State Governments, apex industry chambers, \nAssociations and other organizations taking into consideration their \nviews/comments. \nThe views and sugge stions of various stakeholders were considered \nwhile drafting the above policy document. \n*****  ",
    "ls|LS16_S17_U_167": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 167\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2019\n \n \nPROGRAMMES FOR SENIOR CLASSICAL ARTISTS\n \n \n†\n \n167. SHRI SANJAY KAKA PATIL:\n \n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)  \n \nwhether the Gov\nernment  organizes /proposes to organise programmes in the name \nof artists of national and international repute as a gesture of giving them honour \nand respect, if so, the details thereof; \n \n \n(b)  whether the Government is making any provision to organize pr\nogrammes in the \nname of senior classical artists who devoted their lives to music and for the \nupgradation of music, if so, the details thereof ; \n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government has earmarked /proposes to earmark any funds for the \npurpose; and \n \n \n(d)     if so\n, the details thereof?\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR CULTURE AND MINISTER OF STATE FOR \nENVIORNMENT, FOREST & CLIMATE CHANGE\n \n \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA)\n \n(a & b)\n:\n \nMadam, Ministry of Culture organizes commemoration of Birth Centenary and \nspecial anniversaries like 125\nth\n \n/150\nth\n \netc. of great personalities and memorable \nhistorical events based on the decision of the Government/ respective National \nCommittee, Executive Committee or National Implementation Committee as the case \nmay be.\n \nHowever, Sangeet Natak Akademi, an au\ntonomous organisation under the Ministry \norganized the centenary celebrations of the following Artists throughout the country:\n-\n \n(i)\n     \nSmt \nT. Mukta\n \n(ii)\n    \nBharatratna Smt.\n \nM.S. Subbulakshmi\n \n(iii)\n    \nUstad Bismillah K\nhan\n \n(iv)\n    \nSaint Tyagaraja\n \n(v)\n    \n \nBe\ngum Akhtar\n \n(vi)\n    \nShri Radhika Mohan Maitra \n \n \n(\nc & d ) At present no Specific fund has been earmarked for this purpose\n.\n \n*****\n ",
    "ls|LS16_S17_U_168": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n(MINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS) \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.168 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2019 \n \nWELFARE AND DEVELOPMENT OF TRIBAL POPULATION \n \n168.        SHRI BHARTRUHARI  MAHTAB:  \n                SHRI SANJAY DHOTRE:  \n                SHRI RAHUL SHEWALE: \n \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the schemes/programmes being implemented by the Government for welfare and development of tribal \npopulation in the country;  \n(b)  whether the said schemes/ programmes particularly Tribal Sub -Plan (TSP) have achieved the desired \nresults in the country;  \n (c)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;  \n(d)  the details of the funds provided by the Government under the said schemes/ programmes during each \nof the last three years and the current year;  \n (e)  the cases of irregularities/ corruption/diversion of funds and underutilisation of funds reported under the \nsaid schemes/programmes during the said period along with the action taken/being taken by the \nGovernment thereon; and \n (f)  the other steps taken/being taken by the Government for effective implementation  of the said schemes/ \nprogrammes across the country and to check irregularities/corruption/diversion of funds and under \nutilisation of funds therein? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \n(SHRI JASWANTSINH BHABHOR) \n \n(a): The schemes / programmes being implemented by the Ministry of Tribal Affairs for welfare  and \ndevelopment of tribal population in the country is given at Annexure-I. \n(b)& (c): Tribal Sub-Plan (TSP), now called as Scheduled Tribe Component (STC) at Central level and Tribal \nSub-Scheme (TSS) at State level is dedicated source of fund for tribal development across the country. Fund \nflow under STC / TSS as on date is sourced from various schemes administered by 37 Central Ministries / \nDepartments and State Gove rnments. Major part of infrastructure development and provision of basic \namenities in tribal areas/regions in the country is carried out through various schemes / programmes of \nconcerned Central Ministries and the State Governments, while the Ministry of T ribal Affairs (MoTA) \nprovides by way of additive to these initiatives on plugging gaps. \nSTC/TSS allocations by Central Ministries / Departments:  \nYear Total Plan \nAllocation of \nSTC Ministries \n/ Department \nAllocation Expenditure % Expenditure \nw.r.t. TSS/ STC \nAllocation \n% STC Allocation \nw.r.t. total plan / \nscheme allocation of \nSTC Ministries / \nDepartment \n2014-15 (Actual) 236508.72 19961.29 19436.85 97.37 8.4 \n2015-16 (Actual) 275174.59 20177.62 20024.66 99.24 7.3 \n2016-17 (Actual) 380035.71 24671.58 20956.40 84.94 6.5 \n2017-18 (RE) 443406.47 31292.20 30020.25 95.94 7.1 \n2018-19 (BE) 511402.18 37802.94  21806.86* 57.6 7.4 \n*Upto 18.12.2018. \n\nOverall allocation of STC funds by the concerned Ministries / Department for the year 2018 -19 reached the \nlevel of 7.40 % of their total schematic allocation in these Ministries / Departments. With the efforts of \nMinistry of Tribal Affairs, the trend of STC expenditure has gone up from 85% of total allocation at Revised \nEstimate Stage in 2016-17 to  96%  in 2017-18.  \nFurther, an online system has been put in place by the Ministry of Tribal Affairs (web address \nhttp://stcmis.gov.in) for monitoring of Tribal Sub-Plan(TSP) / Schedule Tribe Component (STC) funds. The \nframework captures data directly from Public Finance Management System (PFMS) and gives inputs to see \nthrough expenditure vis-a- vis allocations.MoTA has been holding periodic meetings with Central Ministries \n/ Departments having STC funds.   Meetings were held on 13.04.2018 and 25.06.2018 followed by another \nreview under the Chairmanship of Secretary on 12/13.07.2018 and 29/30.11.2018. In the said meetings, it has \nbeen reiterated to the Ministries / Departments that STC funds should be as per the prescribed percentage.  \nState TSP: \nMost of the States are allocating TSP funds in proportion to ST population in the State. A statement \nshowing State wise percentage of ST population, total state Plan / Scheme Allocation, TSP Allocation and % \nTSP Allocation for the year 2017-18  is as under: \n(Rs. in Crore) \nS. \nNo. State/U.T \n% \nST   Population \n(2011) \nAnnual   plan 2017-18   \n  \nTotal   State plan / \nScheme  Outlay TSP   Allocation % TSP   Allocation   \n1 Andhra Pradesh 5.5 65208.10 3528.75 5.41   \n2 Assam 12.5 8754.32 1349.72 15.42   \n3 Bihar 1.3 70316.00 1091.50 1.55   \n4 Chhattisgarh 30.6 62462.97 20237.22 32.40   \n5 Goa 10.2 773.80 30.33 3.92   \n6 Gujarat 14.8 172179.24 12559.33 7.29   \n7 Himachal Pradesh 5.7 5700.00 513.00 9.00   \n8 Jammu & Kashmir 11.9  N.R.   N.R. N.R.   \n9 Jharkhand 26.2 44502.61 18092.70 40.66   \n10 Karnataka 7.0 111472.00 8195.00 7.35   \n11 Kerala 1.5 26500.00 751.00 2.83   \n12 Madhya Pradesh 21.1 104358.17 25862.15 24.78   \n13 Maharashtra 9.4 77184.00 6754.00 8.75   \n14 Manipur 35.1 N.R. N.R. N.R.   \n15 Odisha 22.9 55970.00 12564.56 22.45   \n16 Rajasthan 13.5 86094.45 12896.43 14.98   \n17 Sikkim 33.8 N.R. N.R. N.R.   \n18 Tamil Nadu 1.1 54564.19 607.50 1.11   \n19 Telangana 9.1 88038.80 8165.87 9.28   \n20 Tripura 31.8 5572.93 2677.76 48.05   \n21 Uttar Pradesh 0.6 426453.81 577.77 0.14   \n22 Uttarakhand 2.9 42798.31 424.59 0.99   \n23 West Bengal 5.8 60013.89 5039.00 8.40   \n  Total 8.6 1568917.59 141918.18 9.05  \n    N.R.- Not Reported by State Government \n \nAs per  above table that overall TSP / STC Allocation across the country is 9.05% which is  more than \nthe total ST population percentage in the country i.e. 8.6% as per 2011 census. \n \n(d): The details of the funds released by the Ministry under the schemes/ programmes during each of the last \nthree years and the current year is given at Annexure-II. \n(e) & (f): No instance has come to the notice of this Ministry with regard to irregularities/ corruption/diversion \nof funds under any schemes / programmes of the Ministry.  In order to ensure better and effective \n\nimplementation of the schemes across the country and to check irregularities/corruption/diversion of funds \nand under utilisation of funds therein as under:- \n(i) Utilization Certificates are insisted upon as a pre-requisite for further release of funds as per the norms of \nGFR; \n(ii) Progress reports regarding the status of implementation of schemes are obtained. \n(iii) Officers while visiting States/UTs also ascertain the progre ss of implementation of various schemes/ \nprogrammes of the Ministry of Tribal Affairs; \n(iv) Meetings/ Conferences are convened at the Central level with State officials for ensuring timely \nsubmission of proposals, speeding up of implementation of the schem es/ programmes, and reviewing the \nphysical and financial progress; \n(v) Real time data to be uploaded by the State Government on the ministry’s website \nhttps://stcmis.gov.in/smis. \n****** \n   \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \nAnnexure referred in reply to part (a) of  Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.168 for 04.02.2019 raised \nby SHRI BHARTRUHARI  MAHTAB: SHRI SANJAY DHOTRE: SHRI RAHUL SHEWALE , M.P.  \nregarding “Welfare and Development of Tribal Population”. \n \nLIST OF SCHEMES/PROGRAMMES OF THE MINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \nSl.No. Names of Schemes / Programmes \n1 Pre Matric Scholarship Scheme for ST students  \n2 Post Matric Scholarship Scheme for ST students \n3 National Overseas Scholarship for ST students  \n4 National Fellowship and Scholarship for Higher Education of ST students \n5 Grants-in-aid to Voluntary Organisations Working for welfare of STs \n6 Strengthening Education among ST Girls in Low Literacy Districts  \n7 Development of Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Groups (PVTGs)  \n8 Grants under Article 275 (1) of the Constitution of India  \n9 Special Central Assistance(SCA) to Tribal Sub-Scheme(TSS)*  \n10 Gra