    "ls|LS16_S17_U_202": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 202 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2019 \n \nRemoval of Vice Chancellor \n \n202. SHRI MULLAPPALLY RAMACHANDRAN: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Government has received any me morandum from various quarters including from \npublic representatives for removal of the Vice Chancellor of Jawaharlal Nehru University, Delhi; and  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the action taken by the Government thereon? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n \n(DR. SATYA PAL SINGH) \n \n(a) and (b)  Yes, Madam. Representations from public representatives have  been received with \nsome complaints against the Vice Chancellor of Jawaharlal Nehru University (JNU). The Jawaharlal \nNehru University is a statutory autonomous organi zation established under an Act of the Parliament \nand governed by the JNU Act, 1966 and the Statutes  and the Ordinances made thereunder. All \nadministrative and academic decisions are taken by th e University with the approval of its statutory \nbodies, such as Executive Council, Academic Coun cil and Court. The matter was taken up with the \nUniversity which has denied allegations made against the Vice Chancellor.  \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S17_U_203": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \nDEPARTMENT FOR PROMOTION OF INDUSTRY AND INTERNAL TRADE \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 203. \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, THE 4TH FEBRUARY, 2019. \n \nINDUSTRIAL OUTPUT GROWTH \n \n203.  SHRI ANTO ANTONY: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY be pleased to state: \n                                           वाणिज्य एवं उद्योग मंत्री \n(a) whether the Government has noticed the report of decline of Industrial output \ngrowth in the country, if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; \n \n(b) whether the decline of industrial output growth has any adverse impact on the \neconomy; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof and the corrective steps taken by the Government in \nthis regard; and \n \n(d) whether the Government is planning to introduce a new Industrial Policy, if \nso, the details and the salient features thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nवाणिज्‍ य‍एवं‍उद्योग मंत्रालय‍में‍राज्‍ यमंत्री‍(श्री सी.आर.‍चौधरी)‍ \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(SHRI C.R. CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a) to (c):  The Index of Industrial Production (IIP) measures the industrial \nperformance in manufacturing, mining and electricity. As per the latest \nIIP, the industrial output has increased and registered a growth of 5.0 \nper cent during the period April -November 2018-19 as compared to a \ngrowth of 3.2 per cent during the corresponding period of the previous \nyear. \n \n(d) :     The existing Industrial Policy was formulated in the year 1991. In view \nof the rapidly changing landscape of m anufacturing and services, as \nwell as in the light of the new technologies and business models which \nare prevalent now, the need for a new industrial policy to address the \nexisting challenges and take advantage of the future opportunities has \nbeen felt. Th erefore, the Government has decided to formulate a new \nIndustrial Policy which would be a roadmap for all business enterprises \nin the country. In this regard, consultations have been held with \nstakeholders to get their ideas and feedback. Keeping in view a ll such \ninputs, a new Industrial Policy is under consideration of the \nGovernment. \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S17_U_204": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 204 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 4TH FEBRUARY, 2019 \n \nSwachh Vidyalaya Initiative \n \n204. SHRI B. VINOD KUMAR:  \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the number of schools covered by the Gove rnment under its Swachh Vidyalaya Initiative, \nState/UT-wise; \n \n(b)  the distribution of funds a llocated by the Government for the said initiative, State/UT-wise; \n \n(c) whether the inspection of implementation of  said programme has been carried out by the \nGovernment and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d)  whether the Government plan to maintain the infrastructure that has been constructed under the \nsaid initiative and if so, the details thereof along with the budget allocation? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(DR. SATYA PAL SINGH) \n \n(a) The Ministry of Human Resource Development in association with the State Governments, UT \nAdministrations, Public Sector Undertakings (PSUs) and Private Corporates had launched the Swachh \nVidyalaya initiative to provide separate toilets for boys and girls in all government schools within one \nyear. Under this initiative 4,17,796 toilets had been constructed/reconstructed in 2,61,400 Government \nelementary and secondary schools till 15 August, 2015. \n \n(b) The Central Government supports States  and UTs for creation and augmentation of \ninfrastructure facilities including construction of toilets in government elementary and secondary \nschools. State-wise funds alloca ted under erstwhile Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan (SSA) and Rashtriya \nMadhyamik Shiksha Abhiyan (RMSA) schemes exclusiv ely for construction of toilets in government \nschools under the Swachh Vidyalaya initiative are at Annexure-I. PSUs and private corporates \ncontributed in construction of 1,45,102 toilets in government schools with their Corporate Social \nResponsibility funds under the initiative. State-wi se release of funds from Swachh Bharat Kosh for \nconstruction/repair of school toilets under the Swachh Vidyalaya Initiative is at Annexure-II.  \n \n\n(c) & (d) More than 300 Central observers had vis ited the districts across the country to monitor \nthe construction of toilets under the Swachh Vidyala ya initiative. The requirement of infrastructural \nfacilities in schools is worked out every year by the respective State and Union Territory on \nincremental basis depending on the gap identified by UDISE database  and reflected in their Annual \nWork Plan and Budget (AWP&B). Regular meetings ar e held with representatives of States and UTs \nto monitor the infrastructure facilities including toilets in schools. \n \nThe primary responsibility of maintenance of school toilets lies with the concerned State \nGovernment and UT Administrati on. The Ministry of Human Resource Development has requested \nall States/UTs to ensure proper maintenance of toilets constructed under the Swachh Vidyalaya \ninitiative to keep them functiona l, and to also take steps to bring about behavioural changes among \nstudents and teachers through an in tensive awareness campaign to ensure that the toilets are properly \nused and kept neat and clean.  \n \nThe Ministry of Human Resource Development has requested the Public Sector Undertaking \n(PSUs) who participated in the Swachh Vidyalaya initiative to maintain the toilets constructed by \nthem for at least five years.  Further, the Minist ry of Panchayati Raj has advised the Panchayati Raj \nDepartments of all States to incl ude the provision for construction and regular repair/maintenance of \nthe toilets in schools in the Gram Panchayat Development Plans under 14\nth Finance Commission \ngrants. \n \nThe Ministry of Human Resource Development (Department of School Education & Literacy), \nMinistry of Panchayati Raj and Ministry of Drinking Water and Sanitation have also jointly addressed \nthe States regarding participation and support of Gram Panchayats in putting in place proper \narrangements for regular cleaning of school toilets and waste disposal in schools. \n \n A new integrated Scheme for School Educati on - Samagra Shiksha subsuming the erstwhile \nCentrally Sponsored Schemes of SSA, RMSA and Te acher Education (TE) has been launched w.e.f. \n1.4.2018, which inter alia envisages an annual recu rring composite grant of upto Rs. 1.00 lakh, based \non enrolment, for all Government Schools  for  the replacement of non-functional  school  equipment  \nand  for  incurring  other recurring  costs  such  as  consumables for  play  materials,  sports  \nequipment,  laboratories, electricity charges, intern et, water, teaching aids etc. It also provides for \nannual maintenance and repair of existing school building, toilets and other facilities to upkeep the \ninfrastructure in good condition. Each school is requ ired to spend atleast 10% of the composite school \ngrant on activities related to Swachhata Action Plan including maintenance of toilets. \n \n****** \n\n \nANNEXURE-I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 204 TO BE ANSWERED ON 4 TH FEBRUARY, 2019 ASKED BY SHRI B. \nVINOD KUMAR REGARDING “SWACHH VIDYALAYA INITIATIVE”  \n \nState-wise funds allocated/sancti oned for construction and repair of toilets under Swachh Vidyalaya \nInitiatives (SVI) under erstwhile SSA and RMSA schemes. \n \n                ( R s .  i n  l a k h )  \nSl. No. State Name \nFunds allocated (including state share) for \nconstruction/re-construction of toilets under \nSVI \nSSA RMSA \n1 ANDHRA PRADESH 11128.00 2.00\n2 ARUNACHAL PRADESH 1639.20 0.00\n3 ASSAM 18853.61 2991.16\n4 BIHAR 9622.17 535.50\n5 CHHATTISGARH 0.00 232.18\n6 GOA 72.48 0.00\n7 GUJARAT 1210.04 166.94\n8 HARYANA 1380.00 40.25\n9 HIMACHAL PRADESH 52.50 0.00\n10 JAMMU AND KASHMIR 8981.85 