\n3160\n2698\n3210\nNA\n425\n630\n241\n377\nNA\n1907\n2280\n2345\n2511\nNA\nOthers\nNA\nNA\n4600\n2500\n2363\nNA\nNA\nNA\nNA\n1236\nNA\nNA\nNA\nNA\n1064\nAll India\n2462\n2331\n2393\n2525\n2661\n764\n728\n655\n786\n841\n1168\n1075\n968\n1195\n1270\n*4th Advance Estimates., NA: Not Available\nSource: Directorate of Economics & Statistics, DAC&FW\nAnnexure\nState-wise Yield of Cereals, Pulses and Oilseeds\n (Kg/hectare)\nState/UT\nCereals\nPulses\nOilseeds",
    "ls|LS16_S17_U_210": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 210 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 4.2.2019 \n \nNEW KENDRIYA VIDYALAYAS \n†210.  SHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV:  \nSHRI BHEEMRAO B. PATIL:  \nSHRI MANSUKHBHAI DHANJIBHAI VASAVA:  \nSHRI HARISHCHANDRA CHAVAN: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  the details of setting up/ construction of buildings for Kendriya Vidyalayas \n(KVs) in the country, State/UT wise, especially one in Buldhana District of \nMaharashtra;  \n(b)  the total amount allocated to the States for the said purpose including Gujarat \nand Maharashtra for operation of KVs during the last three years, year-wise \nand State/UT-wise;  \n(c)  the number of KVs functioning in all the States, State/UT-wise including Gujarat \nand Maharashtra;  \n(d)  whether the availability of land is a big problem for setting up of new KVs and if \nso, the details thereof along with the action taken by the Government in this \nregard;  \n(e)  whether a lot of requests for setting up of new KVs are pending with \nGovernment; and  \n(f)  if so, the details thereof and the action taken thereon?  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(DR. SATYA PAL SINGH) \n \n(a) Out of 1196 Kendriya Vidyalayas (KVs) functioning in the country 926 are \nfunctioning in permanent buildings and 76 KV buildings are under construction. The \nState / UT- wise details are at Annexure-I.  Kendriya Vidyalaya Sangathan has \ninformed that it has not received any proposal from the Government of Maharashtra \nfor opening of a new KV in Buldhana District.  \n\n(b): The Government of India allocates funds to Kendriya Vidyalaya Sangathan \n(KVS) under the heads Grants-in-Aid-Salar ies, Grant-in-Aid-General and Grants-in-\nAid-Creation of Capital Assets. This allocation is made on a consolidated basis and \nnot State / UT-wise. The deta ils of the grants released to KVS during the last three \nyears are as under:-  \nYear Fund Allocation (Rs. in crores) \n2015-16 3278.47 \n2016-17 3987.25 \n2017-18 4997.25 \n \n(c):  The State/UT-wise details of KVs functioning in the country including Gujarat \nand Maharashtra are at Annexure-II. \n(d) to (f) KVS does not acquire the land for KVs. Suitable piece of land is to be \nprovided by sponsoring authority free of cost to establish a KV. In 25 cases, the \nsponsoring authorities have not transferred the identified land to the respective KVs. \nThis includes 18 cases sponsored by State Governments, 4 by Ministry of Defence \nand 3 by Ministry of Home Affairs.  \nAs regards setting up of new KVs, KVS has informed that it has received 190 \nproposals / requests from various States/UTs for opening of new KVs.  Final decision \nregarding setting up of the KV would depend upon the feasibility report, qualifying \nunder the ‘Challenge Mode’ and approval of the competent authority.  \n*****  \n\nANNEXURE-I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PA RT (a) OF THE LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 210 FOR 4 th FEBRUARY 2019 RAISED BY S HRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV, SHRI \nBHEEMRAO B. PATIL, SHRI MANSUKHBHAI DHANJIBHAI VASAVA ANDSHRI \nHARISHCHANDRA CHAVAN, HON’BLE MEMBER S OF PARLIAMENT REGARDING NEW \nKENDRIYA VIDYALAYAS. \n \nDETAILS OF KENDRIYA VIDYALAYAS WHERE BUILDINGSARE UNDER CONSTRUCTION  \nS. No Name of State / UTs Building under construction  \n1 A&N Island - \n2 Andhra Pradesh 1\n3 Arunachal Pradesh 2\n4 Assam 3\n5 Bihar 5\n6 Chandigarh -\n7 Chhattisgarh 2\n8 Dadra & Nagar \nHaveli \n1\n9 Daman & Diu 1\n10 Delhi 3\n11 Goa -\n12 Gujarat 1\n13 Haryana 3\n14 Himachal Pradesh 3\n15 Jammu & Kashmir 3\n16 Jharkhand -\n17 Karnataka 4\n18 Kerala 5\n19 Lakshadweep 1\n20 Madhya Pradesh 8\n21 Maharashtra 2\n22 Manipur -\n23 Meghalaya -\n24 Mizoram 2\n25 Nagaland -\n26 Odisha 7\n27 Puducherry 1\n28 Punjab -\n29 Rajasthan 4\n30 Sikkim -\n31 Tamil Nadu 2\n32 Talengana 2\n33 Tripura -\n34 Uttar Pradesh 6\n35 Uttrakhand 2\n36 West Bengal 2\nTOTAL\n \n76\n \n***** \n \n\nANNEXURE-II \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (c) OF THE LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 210 FOR 4 th FEBRUARY 2019 RAISED BY SHRI PRATAPRAO \nJADHAV, SHRI BHEEMRAO B. PATIL, SHRI MANSUKHBHAI DHANJIBHAI VASAVA \nANDSHRI HARISHCHANDRA CHAVAN, HON’BLE MEMBERS OF PARLIAMENT \nREGARDING NEW KENDRIYA VIDYALAYAS. \n \nSTATE / UT-WISEKENDRIYA VIDYALAYAS  \nFUNCTIONING IN THE COUNTRY  \n \nSr.  Name of State / UTs Number of KVs \n1 A& N (UT) 02\n2 Andhra Pradesh 33 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 16 \n4 Assam 56 \n5 Bihar 48 \n6 Chandigarh 05 \n7 Chhattisgarh 34 \n8 D&N Haveli 01\n9 Daman & Diu 01 \n10 Delhi 45 \n11 Goa 05 \n12 Gujarat 45 \n13 Haryana 32 \n14 Himachal Pradesh 25 \n15 Jammu & Kashmir 38 \n16 Jharkhand 37 \n17 Karnataka 50 \n18 Kerala 38 \n19 Lakshdweep 01 \n20 Madhya Pradesh 106 \n21 Maharashtra 59 \n22 Manipur 09 \n23 Meghalaya 07 \n24 Mizoram 04 \n25 Nagaland 06 \n26 Odisha 60 \n27 Pondichery 04 \n28 Punjab 50 \n29 Rajasthan 75 \n30 Sikkim 02 \n31 Tamil Nadu 42 \n32 Telangana 35 \n33 Tripura 09 \n34 Uttar Pradesh 113 \n35 Uttarakhand 43\n36 West Bengal 60 \nTOTAL 1196 \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S17_U_211": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.  †211\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2019\n \n \nPROMOTION OF MSMEs\n \n \n†211.\n \n    \nSHRI RAMDAS C. TADAS:\n \n \nWill the Minister of   MICRO,    SMALL    AND    MEDIUM    ENTERPRISES be \npleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \n \nthe steps being taken by the Government to set up Small and Medium Scale \nIndustries;\n \n(b) \n \nthe Special steps taken by the Government to promote Small and Mediu\nm Scale \nIndustries in Maharashtra;\n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government proposes to promote orange industry in the country; and\n \n(d)\n \n \nif so, the details thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)\n \nFOR MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI GIRIRAJ SI\nNGH)\n \n \n \n(a)&(b):\n \nAs per the Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises Development (MSMED) \nAct 2006, the Central Government implements various schemes and programmes for \npromotion and development of micro, small and medium enterprises (MSMEs) throughout \nthe countr\ny including Maharashtra. These include the schemes/programmes such as Prime \nMinister’s Employment Generation Programme (PMEGP), Scheme of Fund for \nRegeneration of Traditional Industries (SFURTI), A Scheme for Promoting Innovation, \nRural Industry and Entrep\nreneurship (ASPIRE), Credit Guarantee Fund Scheme for Micro \nand Small Enterprises, Credit Linked Capital Subsidy Scheme (CLCSS), National \nManufacturing Competitiveness Programme (NMCP), Micro & Small Enterprises \n-\n \nCluster Development Programme (MSE\n-\nCDP), N\national Scheduled Caste and Scheduled \nTribe Hub (NSSH), and schemes for coir products and Khadi & Village Industries.\n \n \nNational Small Industries Corporation (NSIC) under the Ministry of MSME also \npromotes and supports Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises by\n \nproviding integrated \nsupport services encompassing marketing, finance, technology and other services.\n \n \n(c)&(d):\n \n \nMinistry of Agriculture & Farmer’s Welfare and Ministry of Food \nProcessing Industries have the schemes for promotion of fruit and vegetable se\nctor.\n \n \n \n*****\n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S17_U_212": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 212 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2019 \n \nSHARE OF PROFIT IN THE GROSS VALUE ADDED OF THE COUNTRY \n  \n212. SHRI RAM MOHAN NAIDU KINJARAPU:  \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT   be pleased to \nstate: \n  \n(a)whether the Government is aware of the skewed growth in the \nshare of profits in the Gross Value Added (GVA) of the country and if \nso, the details thereof; \n(b)whether steps have been tak en to address the low share of wages \nin the GVA of the country and if so, the details thereof; and \n(c)whether the Government has identified or set any targets for growth \nas well share of wages in the GVA and if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n \n(a):   Central Statistics Office, M inistry of Statistics & \nProgramme Implementation releases estimates of National Income \ncovering various parameters like Net National Income, Net \nDisposable Income of General Government, Net Savings of Public & \nPrivate Corporations, Per Capita Income etc. The figures depicting \nthe performance of such parameters at current prices for 2011 -12 to \n2016-17 are Annexed. \n   However these estimates do not capture the share of \nprofits as such in the Gross Value Added (GVA) of the country. \n(b) & (c): Share of wages is also not captured separately in GVA. The \nCentral Government revises minimum wages for the Scheduled \nemployment from time to time. A notification for revising the minimum \nwages from Rs 250 per day to Rs 350 per day for unskilled workers in \nnon-agriculture sector was issued on 19.01.2017. \n*                                             ******* \n\nAnnexure \nStatement referred to in reply to part (a)  of Lok Sabha unstarred Question No.2                                  \nQuestion No. 212 due for reply on 04-02-2019. \nStatement : Relationship of National Income and Other Aggregates   ( at current prices) \n        \n   \n  \n \n( Rs. in crore) \n    \n \n          \nS.No Item Desription 2011-12 2012-13 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 \n1 2 3 4 5 6 7  8 \n1.        Net National Income 7742330 8766345 9897663 10978237 12154033 13492656 \n2.        Other current \ntransfers from RoW \n(net) \n304902 350081 395918 405154 413083 379438 \n3.        Net National \nDisposable Income \n(1+2) \n8047232 9116426 10293581 11383392 12567117 13872095 \n4.        Net Disposable \nIncome of General \nGovernment \n711518 796476 866932 1002427 1149148 1413811 \n5.        Current taxes on \nincome, wealth, etc.  \n509546 578693 640772 699755 748587 855366 \n6.        Net Saving of Public \nCorporations \n158212 150024 126013 120999 130461 155926 \n7.        Net Saving of Private \nCorporations \n469165 576928 725997 891945 1046193 1134580 \n8.        Current taxes on \nincome and wealth, \npaid by corporations \n356479 388903 389595 439432 463997 524120 \n9.        Personal Income (3-\n4+5-6-7-8) \n6861403 7782788 8825816 9628344 10525902 11499023 \n10.    Current taxes on \nincome and wealth, \npaid by households \n153067 189790 251177 260323 284589 331245 \n11.    Household \nDisposable Income \n(9-10) \n6708336 7592998 8574639 9368021 10241313 11167777 \n \n********* \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S17_U_213": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n   UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 213 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2019 \nSurvey by NCERT \n \n213. SHRI ASADUDDIN OWAISI:          \n \n         Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the National Council of Educati on Research and Training (NCERT) recently \nreleased the National Achievement Survey 2017;  \n(b) if so, the number of districts and students covered under this survey;  \n(c) whether there is a trend of low learning in highe r class as 40% of students can not answer \nquestions in maths, science and social science;  \n(d) if so, the reasons therefor;  \n(e) the extent to which staff cr unch, crowded class rooms and in adequate funds are responsible \nfor low learning in higher classes; and  \n(f) the trend in urban and rural areas shown in the survey and st eps taken or being taken by the \nGovernment in consultation with States in this regard? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(DR. SATYA PAL SINGH) \n \n(a) to (f): National Achievement Survey ( NAS) was conducted on 13th November, 2017 through \nwhich the learning levels of around 22 lakh st udents of Classes III, V and VIII from 1.10 lakh \nschools across 701 districts in all 36  States and UTs were assessed. Students of Classes III and V \nwere assessed on Reading Comprehension, Math ematics and Environmental Studies and Class \nVIII students were assessed on Language, Maths, Sc ience and Social Science. This competency \nbased assessment was based on the Learning Outc omes developed by the National Council of \nEducational Research and Training (NCERT). Along with the test it ems, questionnaires pertaining \nto students, teachers and schools were also used . District report cards for NAS 2017 have been \nreleased and are available on MHRD website.  \n \n\nNAS evidences that the performance of the student s decreases with the advancement of the grade \nin general. The NAS 2017 was conducted on Lear ning Outcomes for the first time. The items \nassessing the performance of the students were all competencies based. NCERT has initiated the \npost NAS activities to improve the Learning levels of the students in rura l and urban areas. The \nshort terms actions include sharing of findings with  State level officers, a nd DIET functionaries in \nregional workshops and Understanding of the l earning gaps in the Districts by the State \nfunctionaries. The medium term goal for NAS in cludes sharing of findings of NAS with Block \nResource Coordinators (BRCs), teachers, head  teachers, parents, SMC members and other \nstakeholders, orientation of BRCs, Cluster Resource Coordinators (CRCs) and teachers on \nevolving learning strategies to en hance learning outcomes at diffe rent grade leve ls, facilitating \nteachers in using alternative instru ctional strategies to bridge the learning gaps with the support of \nState functionaries, and seeking support from community to improve the learning levels.  \n \nDuring 2018-19, many modules for the post NAS interven tions have been initiated which includes \nunderstanding the district report cards, pedagogical inputs to target the learning outcomes, school \nbased assessment, promoting ex periential learning, sports and art integrated education and \nimproving community participation.  \n \nEducation is in the Concurrent List of the C onstitution and a majority of the schools are under the \njurisdiction of respective States an d Union Territories (UT). Hence, the infrastructure and teachers \nrelated aspects of education are primarily in th e domain of respective St ate Governments and UT \nAdministrations. However, the requirement of infr astructural facilities in  schools are worked out \nevery year by the respective State/Union Territory  on incremental basis depending on their need \nand priority and this is reflected in their Annua l Work Plan and Budget. The Central Government \nthrough the Samagra Shiksha scheme provide a ssistance to the States and UTs for school \ninfrastructural facilities and for additional teacher s to maintain appropriate Pupil Teacher Ratio, as \nper norms. Further Samagra Shiksha supports St ates and UTs on early grade reading, writing & \ncomprehension, and early Mathematics programme s through a sub-programme namely ‘Padhe \nBharat Badhe Bharat’ (PBBB) in foundational y ears of schooling. Rashtriya Aavishkar Abhiyan \n(RAA) was also launched to motivate and engage children of the age gr oup from 6-18 years in \nScience, Mathematics and Technology through obser vation, experimentation, inference drawing, \nmodel building, etc. both through inside and outside classroom activities. \n \n **** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S17_U_214": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 214 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2019 \n \nCentral Universities  \n \n214. PROF. SAUGATA ROY:  \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has sanctioned crores of rupees of central funds for 13 new \nCentral Universities in the country;  \n \n(b) if so, the details of such universities and the amount sanctioned to each university; \n \n(c) whether the central assistance would help such universities to increase the access to higher \neducation and minimise the regional imbalances in educational facilities;  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(e) whether there is any request for setting up of new Central University in the country; and  \n \n(f) if so, the details thereof and the action taken by the Government in this regard? \n  \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n (Dr. SATYA PAL SINGH) \n \n(a) and (b): Yes, Madam. The Cabinet has approved the Revised Cost Estimates (RCE) of the \n13 new Central Universities (CUs) establishe d under CU Act, 2009 which inter-alia include \n(i) incurring an expenditure of Rs. 3639.32 crore for recurring and creation of necessary \ninfrastructure for the period from 2018-19 to 2020-21and (ii) ex-post facto approval granted \nfor Rs. 1474.65 crores being the amount spent over and above the earlier approval of the \nCabinet for Rs. 3000 crores for these Central Universities. The University-wise details of the \nsanctioned amount to these 13 Central Universities are annexed.  \n \n(c) and (d): These  13 new Central Universities have been established under Central \nUniversities Act, 2009, in the uncovered States like Bihar, Gujarat, Haryana, Himachal \nPradesh, Jammu & Kashmir, Jharkhand, Karnat aka, Kerala, Orissa, Punjab, Rajasthan and \nTamil Nadu, with a view to increasing the acce ss and quality of higher education  as well as \nminimizing the regional imbalances in educational facilities.  \n \n(e) and (f): At present there is no proposal to set up new Central Universities in the country. \nHowever, the Andhra Pradesh Re-organization Act 2014 envisages establishment of one \nCentral Tribal University each in the State of Andhra Pradesh and Telangana and one Central \nUniversity in the State of Andhra Pradesh. Fu rther, the Prime Minister announced a package \nfor Bihar on 18.08.2015 which includes establishment of a Central University near Bhagalpur \nat the historic site of Vikramshila University. \n \n***** \n\n \nAnnexure \n \nAnnexure as referred to in reply to parts (a) and (b) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. \n214 for 04.02.2019 regarding Central Universities \n \n(Rs. in crores) \nS.No. Name of Central \nUniversity \nExpenditure already \nincurred   \nAmount  sanctioned \nfor 2018-19 to 2020-21 \n1.  CU, South Bihar 321.90 305.11 \n2.  CU,  Jammu 419.36 276.12 \n3.  CU,  Jharkhand 299.63 420.97 \n4.  CU,  Kashmir 147.36 518.67 \n5.  CU, Orissa 175.17 306.07 \n6.  CU,  Kerala 439.41 278.11 \n7.  CU, Punjab 267.50 510.38 \n8.  CU,  Haryana 417.17 156.35 \n9.  CU,  Rajasthan 647.12 173.39 \n10.  CU,  Karnataka 475.50 158.90 \n11.  CU , Tamil Nadu 536.99 181.46 \n12.  CU,  Gujarat 213.38 178.28* \n13.  CU,  Himachal Pradesh 114.16 175.51* \nTotal 4474.65 3639.32 \n \n* This amount is only the recurring component fo r Central Universities of Gujarat and Himachal \nPradesh. The capital funds have not been sanctioned to  these Central Universities as they have not yet \nstarted the construction work   for permanent camp us due to non-allotment of land by the respective \nState Governments.  \n \n____________ \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S17_U_215": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.†215 \nANSWERED ON 04.02.2019 \n \nSETTING UP OF RURAL TOURISM HUBS \n \n†215. SHRI JUGAL KISHORE:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government proposes to set up rural tourism hubs and \ntourism parks to promote tourism on a large scale;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c)  the details of such hubs and parks proposed to be set up in Jammu \nand Kashmir along with the number thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(SHRI K.J. ALPHONS) \n \n(a) & (b): The Ministry of Tourism, under the scheme of Swadesh \nDarshan, provides Central Financial Assistance to State \nGovernments/Union Territory (UT) Administrations/ Central Agencies for \ndevelopment of tourist infrastructure in identified thematic circuits in the \ncountry. The projects for development under the scheme are identified in \nconsultation with the State Governments/UT Administrations and are \nsanctioned subject to availability of funds, s ubmission of suitable detailed \nproject reports, adherence to scheme guidelines and utilization of funds \nreleased earlier. \n \nRural circuit is one of the fifteen thematic circuits identified for \ndevelopment under the scheme. The list of projects sanctioned by  the \nMinistry under the Rural Circuit is as below:    \n(Rs. in crore)  \nS. \nNo. \nState/Year \nof Sanction \nName of the Project Amount \nSanctioned \n1. Bihar \n2017-18 \nDevelopment of Bhitiharwa - \nChandrahia - Turkaulia   \n44.65 \n2. Kerala  \n2018-19 \nDevelopment of Malana d Malabar \nCruise Tourism \n80.37 \n          \n(c): The list of projects sanctioned to the State of Jammu and Kashmir \nunder the Swadesh Darshan Scheme is given at the Annexure. No project \nhas been sanctioned to the state under Rural Circuit of the above scheme.  \n*******\n\nANNEXURE \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART (c) OF L OK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. †215 ANSWERED ON 04.02.2019 REGARDING SETTING \nUP OF RURAL TOURISM HUBS. \n \nThe list of projects sanctioned to the State of Jammu and Kashmir under \nthe Swadesh Darshan Scheme of Ministry of Tourism is as below: \n(Rs. in crore) \nSl. \nNo. \nName of \nthe Circuit \nName of the Project Amt. \nSanctioned \nYear 2016-17 \n1. Himalayan \nCircuit \nIntegrated Development of Tourism \nInfrastructure Projects in the State of \nJammu and Kashmir.  \n82.97 \n2. Himalayan \nCircuit \nIntegrated Development of Tourist \nFacilities at Jammu -Rajouri-Shopian-\nPulwama  under Himalayan Circuit \ntheme in J&K. \n96.38 \n3. Himalayan \nCircuit \nIntegrated Development of Tourist \nFacilities under the Construction of \nAssets in lieu of thos e Destroyed in \nFloods in 2014 under PM Development \nPackage for J&K \n98.70 \n4. Himalayan \nCircuit \nIntegrated Development of Tourist \nfacilities at Mantalai - Sudhmahadev- \nPatnitop under Himalayan Circuit Theme \nin J&K. \n97.82 \n \n5. Himalayan \nCircuit \nIntegrated Dev elopment of Tourist \nFacilities at Anantnag - Kishtwar- \nPahalgam – Daksum – Ranjit Sagar Dam \nunder Himalayan Circuit Theme in J&K. \n96.39 \n6. Himalayan \nCircuit \nIntegrated Development of Tourist \nFacilities at Gulmarg -Baramulla-\nKupwara-Leh Circuit under Himalay an \nCircuit Theme in J&K. \n96.93 \n  Total 569.19 \n \n \n******** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S17_U_216": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †216 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 4th FEBRUARY, 2019 \n \nCBSE Affiliation Laws \n†216. SHRI UDAY PRATAP SINGH: \n \n Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether the Central Board of Secondary Education (CBSE) has revised \n affiliation bye-laws rules recently;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) whether the Government proposes to take any concrete steps or enact any \n concrete law in view of the arbitrary attitude of private schools; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(DR. SATYA PAL SINGH) \n**** \n(a) and (b): The Central Board of Secondary Education (CBSE) has notified its \nrevamped Affiliation Bye-Laws vide their Notification No. CBSE /Aff./Circular/10/ \n2018/1424692 dated 18.10.2018. A copy of same is available on the Board's website \nwww.cbseaff.nic.in\n.  The revamped Affiliation Bye-Laws depict a paradigm shift from the \nhighly complex procedure followed earlier to  a simplified system based on preventing \nduplication of processes, speedy transparent and hassle free procedures and ensuring \nthe ease of doing business with CBSE. The en tire affiliation process will be completed \nonline and integrated with instantaneous communication. The following are some of the \nmajor changes in the revised Affiliation Bye-Laws:-  \n \n Instead of 12-14 documents, a single certificate from District Education Officer \nand a single affidavit is required. \n \n The process of Inspection of schools is based on outcome based methodology \nfocusing on quality of education.  \n \n Standardization of measurement of Land is done by changing from acreage to \nmetric system. \n \n\n The requirements of affiliation have be en clearly defined as essential, post-\naffiliation and general. Now schools will be required to fulfill certain criteria like \nrecruitment of teachers etc., post affiliation, but before commencement of session. \n \n Well defined provisions for Shifting of school, Change of name of school/Society, \nTransfer from one society and closure of school etc. to avoid any variation in the \nprocessing of various requests are added.  \n \n Relaxation is given in the requirements of land given for states of Arunachal \nPradesh, Sikkim and Andaman & Nicobar/Lakshadweep Islands, being difficult \nareas with scarce land resources, at par with Metro Cities. \n \n The lease period has been reduced from 30 to 15 years with ownership guarantee \nof 5 years only on the date of application. \n \n The responsibilities of various functionaries have been defined in clear terms with \nadequate penal provisions. \n  \n As per the revamped affiliation bye-laws the entire affiliation process will be \ncompleted online and integrated with instantaneous communication which will ensure \ntransparency in the affiliation process. \n \n(c) and (d): The Board has incorporated vari ous provisions in revamped Affiliated Bye-\nLaws for the school's to be followed to avoid arbitrary action on part of the private \nschools.  \n***** \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S17_U_217": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.†217 \nANSWERED ON 04.02.2019 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF RELIGIOUS PLACES IN TRIBAL AREAS \n \n†217. SHRI ASHOK MAHADEORAO NETE:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government has taken/proposes to take any steps \nto develop religious places Devsthan situated in tribal \ndominated areas of the country as tourist places especially in \nMaharashtra;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof;  \n(c)  whether the Government proposes to i nclude the religious \nplace of markhanda devsthan in Chamorshi Taluka under \nGadchiroli-Chimur parliamentary constituency of Maharashtra \nin the central list of tourists spots and develop this religious \nplace situated in tribal area as tourist destination; and  \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(SHRI K.J. ALPHONS) \n \n(a) & (b): Development of religious places is the responsibility of \nrespective State Governments/Union Territories. Ministry of \nTourism, un der the schemes viz., Pilgrimage Rejuvenation and \nSpiritual, Heritage Augmentation Drive (PRASHAD) and “Integrated \nDevelopment of Theme Based Tourist Circuit in the Country, \nSwadesh Darshan (SD)\" only provides Central Financial Assistance \nfor infrastructure development and beautification of tourist spots on \nreceipt of suitable DPRs submitted by State Governments/Union \nTerritories, subject to availability of funds, liquidation of pending \nutilisation certificate against the fund released earlier and \n\nadherence to the relevant scheme guidelines. However, details of \nplaces/destinations selected for development including Tribal \ndominated areas under PRASHAD Scheme of the Ministry, in \nconsultation with the respective State Governments is annexed. \n \n(c) & (d): No suc h proposal for development of religious place in \ntribal dominated areas in Maharashtra including Markhanda \nDevsthan in Chamorshi Taluk under Gadchiroli –Chimur \nParliamentary Constituency of Maharashtra has been received from \nState Government of Maharashtra .   However, for the State of \nMaharashtra, Ministry of Tourism has approved the project \n“Development of Trimbakeshwar” in the year 2017 -18 at the cost of \nRs.37.81 Crore under PRASHAD Scheme. \n \n******** \n\nANNEXURE \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART S (a) & (b) OF L OK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †217 ANSWERED ON 04.02.2019 \nREGARDING DEVELOPMENT OF RELIGIOUS PLACES IN TRIBAL \nAREAS \n \nDetail of religious places/destinations in Tribal dominated areas \nselected for development under PRASHAD Scheme. \nSl. \nNo. \nStates Selected Religious places \n1 Arunachal Pradesh   Parshuramkund Temple, Lohit District \n2 Tripura  Tripura Sundari Temple in Agartala \n3 Meghalaya  Charantala Durga Temple, West Garo \nHills District \n Nartiang Temple, West Jaintia Hills \nDistrict,  \n Nongsawlia Presbyterian Chu rch, East \nKhasi Hills District \n Aitnar Sacred Pool for Behdeinkhlam \nFestival, West Jaintia Hills District \n4 Mizoram  Aizwal District for development at \nDurtlong Hill Monuments,  \n Kidron Valley, Dawrpui Old Church,  \n Khawruhlian, Baktawng, Changsilkai \nand Serkaw in Siaha District \n5 Nagaland  St. Mary’s Cathedral, Kohima District \n Molungkimong, Mokokchung, District  \n Aizuto, Zunheboto District  \n Noksen, Tunesang District \n6 Chhattisgarh Maa Bamleswhwari Devi Temple, \nDongargarh, District Rajnandgaon \n7 Himachal Pradesh Maa Chintpurni Temple, District Una. \n8 Haryana Gurudwara Nada Saheb, Panchkula \n9 Karnataka Chamundesheari Devi Temple, Mysuru \n10 Kerala  Guruvayur Temple \n St. Thomas International Shrine, \nMalayattoor \n Cheraman JUma Mosque, Thrissur \n11 Madhya Pradesh   Amarkantak \n Omkareshwar \n12 Andhra   Amaravati \n\nPradesh   Srisailam \n13 Uttarakhand  Kedarnath \n Badrinath \n Gangotri  \n Yamunetri \n14 Bihar  Vishnupad Temple, Gaya \n Patna Sahib \n15 Assam   Kamakhya Temple, Guwahati \n16 Jammu & \nKashmir \n Hazratbal, Srinagar \n Katra, Jammu \n17 Gujrat  Dwarka \n Somnath \n18 Maharashtra  Trimbakeshwar \n19 Uttar Pradesh  Varanasi \n Mathura \n20 Tamil Nadu  Kancheepuram \n Vellankani \n21 Jharkhand  Baba Baidyanathji Dham, Deoghar \n Parasnath \n22 Odisha  Puri \n23 Rajasthan  Ajmer-Pushkar \n24 West Bengal  Belur math \n25 Punjab  Karunasagar, Amritsar \n \n****** ",
    "ls|LS16_S17_U_218": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.218 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2019 \n \nForeign Universities \n218. SHRI K. PARASURAMAN:  \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government is aware that Doctoral degree holders from the top 500 \nforeign universities are now eligible for direct recruitment as an Assistant Professor in \nIndian varsities;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c) whether it would help to cater to the growing demand for quality higher education within \nthe country and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(DR. SATYA PAL SINGH) \n(a)to (c):Yes, Madam. As per University Gran ts Commission (Minimum Qualifications for \nAppointment of Teachers and other Academic Staff in Universities and Colleges and other \nMeasures for the Maintenance of Standards in Higher Education) Regulations, 2018, a \nperson with Ph.D. degree from a foreign university/institution with a ranking among top \n500 in the World University Ranking (at any time) by any one of the following: (i) \nQuacquarelli Symonds (QS) (ii) the Times High er Education (THE) or (iii) the Academic \nRanking of World Universities (ARWU) of the Shanghai Jiao Tong University (Shanghai) is \neligible for direct recruitment as Assistant Professor in universities and colleges. The said \nRegulations is intended to attract and retain the best quality teachers and to encourage \nresearch in Universities and Colleges. The aforesaid regulations are available on UGC \nwebsite at https://www.ugc.ac.in/pdfnews/4033931_UGC-\nRegulation_min_Qualification_Jul2018.pdf. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S17_U_219": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.219 \nANSWERED ON 04.02.2019 \n \nTOURISM PROJECTS UNDER BHARAT MALA PROGRAMME \n \n219.  SHRI J.C. DIVAKAR REDDY:  \nSHRI KONAKALLA NARAYANA RAO:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government of Andhra Pradesh (A.P.) has \nrequested to give more importance to the tourism projects of \nVisakhapatnam and Konaseema regions under Bharat Mala \nProgramme;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and the action taken thereon along  \nwith the places identified for the purpose;  \n(c)  whether the Government proposes to increase tourism by \njoining the connectivity to ports and Vodarevu in A.P. to \nimprove tourism in Itchapuram, Bhogapuram, Bhimili, \nVisakhapatnam, Kakinada, Narsapur, Nizam patnam, Ongole, \nRamayyapatnam, Duggarajapatnam and in all other tourist \nspots/areas in A.P. in future and if so, the details thereof;  \n(d)  the steps taken/being taken by the Union and State \nGovernments to include all the districts which are having \ncoastal areas and potential for improving tourism facilities \nunder Bharat Mala Programme; and  \n(e)  the details of the coastal corridors being developed including \nTada to Itchapuram in A.P. for tourism purpose? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(SHRI K.J. ALPHONS) \n \n(a) & (b): The Ministry of Road Transport and Highways has  \ninformed that no such request regarding tourism projects under \nBharatmala Programme has been received in Bharatmala Pariyojana \n\n& Special Projects Cell (M/o RT&H) from Government of Andhra \nPradesh.   \n \nHowever, development of Malkaniri on National Highway -326 \nChintapalle- Naripattanam –Waddai-Chodavaram- Sabbavaram-\nGandigudan National Highway-16 near Tallavalasa stretch and route \nstarting from National Highway -565- Gotlagattu-Podile- Uppalapadu- \nChimakurti-Ongloe on National Highway -16 within an aggregate \nlength of about 350 kms in the state of Andhra Pradesh has been \nenvisaged under Backward Districts, Religious and Tourist Centres \nConnectivity. \n \n(c): Ministry of Road Tra nsport and Highways is implementing five \nprojects connecting National Highway -16 to Vizag, Kakinada Ports \nin Visakhapatnam & East Godavari Districts which is at Annexure. \n \n(d): Ministry of Road Transport and Highways is implementing six \nprojects connecting NH-16 to the ports in other districts. \n \n(e): Ministry of Road Transport and Highways is implementing \ntwelve projects on National Highway -16 from Ichhapuram to Tada \ncovering coastal Districts of Andhra Pradesh.   \n \n******** \n \n\nANNEXURE \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY T O PART (c) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO.219 ANSWERED ON 04.02.2019 REGARDING \nTOURISM PROJECTS UNDER BHARAT MALA PROGRAMME. \n \nTHE DETAILS OF THE PROJECT CONNECTING NH -16 TO VIZAG, \nKAKINADA PORTS IN VISHAKHAPATNAM & EAST GODAVARI \nDISTRICT. \n \nSl. \nNo. \nName of the Projects Length \n(Km.) \n1 Development of adequate road connectivity to \nVisakhapatnam Port to NH-16 (Phase II works) \n4.15 \n2 Four lane connectivity to Visakhapatnam Port \nfrom Km.0.000 to Km.12.700 (Design Length= \n12.70 Km) in Andhra Pradesh under Por t \nConnectivity under Bharatmala Pariyojana on \nHybrid Annuity Mode \n12.7 \n3 Construction of Six laning of Road Connecting to \nNH-16 at Gajuwaka to Gangavaram Port from Km \n0.00 to Km 4.088 and Four laning of Road \nConnecting Gangavaram Port to Beach Road from \nKm 4.088 to Km 11.500 in Visakhapatnam District \n(Package-I) \n10.34 \n4 Development of Four lane of Achampeta Junction \nto Vakalpudi Light House (Old Port Junction) road \nin East Godavari District \n13.6 \n5 Four laning of Kakinada Anchorage Port Uppada \nbeach road connection upto NH -16 in East \nGodavari District \n43 \n \n********* \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S17_U_22": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 22 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2019 \n \nVocationalisation of Higher Secondary Education \n \n22. DR. HEENA VIJAYKUMAR GAVIT:  \n      SHRI  MOHITE PATIL VIJAYSINH SHANKARRAO:  \n      SHRI DHANANJAY MAHADIK:  \n      SHRI SATAV RAJEEV:  \n      SHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE:  \n      DR. J. JAYAVARDHAN:  \n      SHRIMATI P.K. SREEMATHI TEACHER:  \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is implement ing a revised centrally sponsored of \nVocationalisation of Higher Secondary Education (VHSE) in the country;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the aims and objectives of the schemes;  \n(c) whether the Government is facing any hindrances to implement the said curriculum;  \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the measures taken by the Government in this regard; and  \n(e) whether the Government is considering fo r introduction of any continuous evaluation \nsystem for teacher to maintain or upgrade their efficiency and if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(DR. SATYA PAL SINGH) \n(a) to (d): The erstwhile Centrally Sponsor ed Scheme of Vocationalis ation of Secondary \nand Higher Secondary Education  has b een revised and subsumed under Samagra \nShiksha – an integrated scheme for sc hool education and is now known as \nVocationalisation of School Education. The component of Vocationalisation of School \nEducation under Samagra Shiksha seeks to integrate vocational education with general \nacademic education with an aim to prepare educ ated, employable and competitive human \nresource for various sectors of the economy and the global market. The main objectives of \n\nthe component of Vocationalisation of School Education are to enhanc e the employability \nof youth through demand driven competency based,  modular vocational courses, to bridge \nthe divide between the academic and applied learning and reduce the dropout rate in \nschools. Under this component, NSQF compli ant vocational courses are taught to the \nstudents from class 9th to 12th.  At the secondary level i.e. class 9th and 10th, vocational \ncourses are offered to the students as an additiona l subject. At Senior Secondary level, i.e. \nclass XI and XII, vocational courses are offe red as a compulsory (elective) subject. \nCurriculum and course material is developed by  Pandit Sunderlal Sharma Central Institute \nof Vocational Education in consultation with the Sector Skill Councils. So far the scheme \nhas been implemented in 7470 schools. \n \n(e): National Council of Educational Res earch and Training (NCERT) has developed \nguidelines along with tool for assessing the performance of teachers titled Performance \nIndicators for Elementary School Teachers (PINDICS). PINDICS can be used for self-\nassessment by the teachers for their improvem ent. It can also be used by Head Teacher/ \nPrincipal/Block Resource Center for assess ing the performance of the teachers and to \nprovide professional support through appropriate teacher development programme/training. \nPINDICS mobile app on google pl ay store and online version ar e also available. NCERT \nhas shared this with all Stat es/UTs and provided academic support to States/UTs for its \nimplementation.  \n \nUnder Samagra Shiksha - Inte grated Scheme for School Educat ion, support is given to \nState Governments and UT Admini strations on several interventions to improve teaching \nstandards, including inter alia regular in-service  teachers’ training, induction training for \nnewly recruited teachers, training on ICT Component, Inclusive Education, Gender \nSensitization and Adolescent Education and remedial teac hing for academically weaker \nstudents. Under Samagra Shiksha, the focus is to give subject specific, need based and \nrelevant in-service teacher training to both elementary and secondary teachers for their \nprofessional development. The trainings are provided thr ough the institutional  structure of \nState Council of Educationa l Research and Training (SCERT ) at State level, District \nInstitute of Education and Trai ning (DIET) at District level and Block Resource Centers / \nCluster Resource Centers at Block/Cluster level.  \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S17_U_220": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 220 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2019 \n \nEMPLOYMENT TO TRAINED PERSONS \n \n†220. SHRI BHAIRON PRASAD MISHRA: \n          SHRI AJAY MISRA TENI: \n       \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP be \npleased to state: \n \n(a) the number of persons trained in various Skill Development Centres from 2014 to \nDecember, 2018, State/UT-wise;  \n \n(b)  the number of people provided employment after  getting training from Skill \nDevelopment and Entrepreneurship Training Centres during the said period; and  \n \n(c)  the target fixed for providing employment to t hose who have not been provided \nemployment so far despite providing training?  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI ANANTKUMAR HEGDE) \n \n(a) & (c) Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepr eneurship through National Skill \nDevelopment Corporation is implementing its flagshi p scheme Pradhan Mantri Kaushal \nVikas Yojana (PMKVY) which was launched in 2015. Th e Ministry is also promoting \nestablishment of model and aspirational skill centr es known as Pradhan Mantri Kaushal \nKendra (PMKK) in every district for imparting skill  training. State wise details of number \nof people provided short term training (STT) and of fered placement under the scheme \nare at Annexure I.  Owing to the success of PMKVY 1.0, the scheme was re-launched in \nOctober, 2016 and is called PMKVY 2016-20.   State wise details of number of people \ntrained and placed under various components of PMKVY 2.0 are at Annexure II.  \n \n   \n \n Under the scheme, focus on employment has been sig nificantly enhanced and \ncandidates have been placed in various sectors and industries. Training Centers (TCs)/ \nTraining Providers (TPs) are mandated to organize p lacement/rozgar melas every six \nmonth with the support of Sector Skill Council. Sch eme also incentivizes TCs/TPs for \nfacilitating placement of the trained candidates. A lso, the reimbursement of last 20% of \ntraining cost to TCs would be made only after wage employment or self employment to \n70% of the trained candidates. Additionally, post p lacement support is applicable for \nspecial group (women, candidates below poverty line  and persons with disability) and \nspecial areas (Left Wing Extremism, North East regi on and J&K) for 2 or 3 month post \ntraining depending on placement within or outside the domicile district of the candidate.  \n\nAnnexure I \n  \nDetails of candidates benefitted under PMKVY 1.0 from 2015-16 to 2017-18 \n \n  \nS.No.   State Total Trained Total placed Certified under R PL \n1 Andaman and Nicobar Islands 194 0   \n2 Andhra Pradesh 129611 18642 4627 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 1017 88   \n4 Assam 31154 3694 1583 \n5 Bihar 89252 12047 1758 \n6 Chandigarh 4851 396 81 \n7 Chhattisgarh 36488 1351 307 \n8 Dadra and Nagar Haveli  258  96    \n9 Daman and Diu 230 111   \n10 Delhi 75194 5151 17161 \n11 Goa 499 213   \n12 Gujarat 43324 3152 448 \n13 Haryana 81885 8456 3033 \n14 Himachal Pradesh 22738 2128 74 \n15 Jammu and Kashmir 17779 274 229 \n16 Jharkhand 26758 1855 1606 \n17 Karnataka 73607 13877 1736 \n18 Kerala 14689 1487 465 \n19 Madhya Pradesh 159535 21636 6549 \n20 Maharashtra 84455 10844 17676 \n21 Manipur 1328 499 157 \n22 Meghalaya 1701 110 29 \n23 Mizoram 1030 93   \n24 Nagaland 1271 77   \n25 Odisha 56822 10430 2650 \n26 Puducherry 7070 904 181 \n27 Punjab 72531 10616 9039 \n28 Rajasthan 113162 13224 16146 \n29 Sikkim 886 13   \n30 Tamil Nadu 151570 44749 13433 \n31 Telangana 98653 20923 6658 \n32 Tripura 14018 5235 782 \n33 Uttar Pradesh 259004 24037 5078 \n34 Uttarakhand 13675 1180 234 \n35 West Bengal 118052 14320 7306 \n \n         36 Grand Total 1804110 251895 119026 \n\nAnnexure II \n  \nDetails of candidates benefitted under PMKVY 2.0 from 2016 till 24.01.2019 \n \n   STT Spe